{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfC2qWKsU8o61bsFg8Fv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final bundle passes everything I checked: the Settings mouse click works, the game-over and About screens render, there are no page errors, and the 2-player hub check passes. Now I'll write the final `exit-interview.json` with the feedback answers, then rebuild so the About screen carries the same text."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1369,"cache_read_input_tokens":215232,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1369,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41d7e7a4-7a0c-4c1d-8249-64635219af5f","uuid":"8e81e2ec-e40c-459e-afae-6592f37354c5","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:17.438Z","request_id":"req_011CgfC2q6mdwuwT7yVUTpuV"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfC2qWKsU8o61bsFg8Fv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N3pfzSUkJ7FTqcEZ5h889X","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-02 19:02\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008), created by David Simon and Ed Burns\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth, with a listening minigame (adventure)\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Third-person 3/4 camera on the Corner (V cycles to the side view); side view in the Wire Room\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 engine (handoff package, one file, no dependencies); AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.3.0\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native pixel art on a PS1-style low-resolution buffer: brick rowhouses, a stash-house roof, streetlamps, dusk lighting\",\n    \"animation\": \"Engine-native skeletal rigs (E.Humanoid), telegraphed attacks (E.Attack), takedown and parry actions\",\n    \"audio\": \"All code-written: chip-style songs in layers for the Corner and the Wire Room, synth sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices pitched per speaker\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub parallel mode (1-4 players, each on their own copy, scored on time). No other network use\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless Chromium (Playwright) for the flow, scoring and screens, the WebKit phone profiles via viewport-matrix, and the AI Hub harness for multiplayer (2 players, and 4 with latency, jitter and loss)\",\n    \"research\": \"Read the engine handoff docs (API card, kits README, the stealth kit source) and the AI Hub session protocol README\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\n    \"usage\": \"unknown\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"Nine issues: five found by reading the code before the first run, four by the browser runs (a glyph that rendered as a question mark, a line that could not be flagged until its typing finished, the About screen opening over an open results panel, and the touch-only title still showing the keyboard hint). Also one harness failure that was not the game's: another agent's server held the port the harness used.\",\n    \"One critical issue, caught in review before any run: the Wire Room would have thrown on entry, because its helper was scoped inside the Corner. It was fixed; no player would have reached it.\",\n    \"The most helpful test was the AI Hub harness: it names every protocol check, and it passed for 2 players and for 4 players with latency, jitter and packet loss.\",\n    \"The least helpful test was the timed screenshot wait for attract mode: it took twelve seconds to show what the code already covered, and the Settings screenshot missed the Controller group until a DOM check.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"The stealth kit was the best part: guards with sight cones, takedowns and parries, and a HUD in one readable file. The Shell's results and settings screens, and the E.Dialog choices, worked without extra code.\",\n    \"I guessed on the stick inversion, since the settings screen has no axis option: I defined the toggles and wrapped the input in a proxy that inverts only pad sticks. I also guessed the weapon name for the guards and used 'sword', because the API card has no list of weapon names. E.Shell screens also stay open across scene changes, so the About screen opened over the results panel until I closed it by hand.\",\n    \"The most important things I built myself: the Wire Room minigame, the Animalese voices (a blip per letter, pitched per speaker), the scrolling About screen with the feedback text, the attract-mode route, and the AI Hub round glue.\",\n    \"The hardest part was the size: a 1.2 MB single file and a 66 KB API card. I read the stealth kit closely to learn the patterns, and a full clone of the repository filled the temporary disk, so I had to fetch only the handoff folder.\",\n    \"I would overhaul the Shell's screen lifetime: a screen should close when its scene changes, or the scene should be able to close it with one call.\",\n    \"I would add a built-in stick-inversion option to the Controller settings group, and a short list of the rig's weapon names in the API card.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The done criteria and the exit-interview shape were precise, and the links to the engine and hub README let me work from the real protocol rather than a guess.\",\n    \"The folder rules were unclear: the brief says to create the subfolder 'here' and to bundle the HTML 'in root', and I read 'root' as the work folder, where I put the HTML and the JSON. The brainstorm line says 50 titles and I produced 52. The Usage field asks for cost and token estimates that I could not measure.\",\n    \"The hardest thing was fitting the whole list into one pass: the controller-first UI with glyph swaps, attract mode, touch-only handling, the Wire Room, the multiplayer round, and the test tools. I did not test on a real gamepad or on real iOS Safari.\",\n    \"The test I would most like to run is a real DualSense in Chrome, to confirm that the two inversion toggles and the glyph swap feel right, followed by a four-player round across separate machines.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Wireframe Hour\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Stealth on the Corner. Listening in the Wire Room.\",\n    \"description\": \"A three-day Baltimore demo: steal a notebook off a stash-house roof past the corner boys, then flag the real lines in the wire room. Animalese voices, a choice that follows you, and a short timed round on the hub.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick or arrows move, Space jump, J strike, K block, C crouch, Shift sprint, L roll\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3d\", \"stealth\", \"retro\", \"exploration\", \"versus\", \"comedy\"]\n  }\n}\nEOF\nnode -e '\nconst j=JSON.parse(require(\"fs\").readFileSync(\"exit-interview.json\",\"utf8\"));\nconst empties=[]; const walk=(o,p)=>{ if(typeof o===\"string\"){ if(!o.trim() && p!==\"aiHub.series\") empties.push(p);} else if(Array.isArray(o)) o.forEach((x,i)=>walk(x,p+\"[\"+i+\"]\")); else if(o&&typeof o===\"object\") for(const k in o) walk(o[k],p?p+\".\"+k:k); };\nwalk(j,\"\"); console.log(\"parses; empty fields:\", empties.length?empties:\"none\"); console.log(\"tags:\", j.aiHub.tags.length, \"genre ok:\", [\"Stealth\"].includes(j.aiHub.genre));' && node build.mjs | tail -1","description":"Write final exit-interview JSON, validate it, and rebuild"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1369,"cache_read_input_tokens":215232,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1369,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41d7e7a4-7a0c-4c1d-8249-64635219af5f","uuid":"a9337ee0-4b50-4a75-ac05-189d0021ec98","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:26.618Z","request_id":"req_011CgfC2q6mdwuwT7yVUTpuV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01N3pfzSUkJ7FTqcEZ5h889X":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium__20261102-190205__xac1bf5/work && cat > exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-02 19:02\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008), created by David Simon and Ed Burns\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth, with a listening minigame (adventure)\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Third-person 3/4 camera on the Corner (V cycles to the side view); side view in the Wire Room\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 engine (handoff package, one file, no dependencies); AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.3.0\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native pixel art on a PS1-style low-resolution buffer: brick rowhouses, a stash-house roof, streetlamps, dusk lighting\",\n    \"animation\": \"Engine-native skeletal rigs (E.Humanoid), telegraphed attacks (E.Attack), takedown and parry actions\",\n    \"audio\": \"All code-written: chip-style songs in layers for the Corner and the Wire Room, synth sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices pitched per speaker\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub parallel mode (1-4 players, each on their own copy, scored on time). No other network use\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless Chromium (Playwright) for the flow, scoring and screens, the WebKit phone profiles via viewport-matrix, and the AI Hub harness for multiplayer (2 players, and 4 with latency, jitter and loss)\",\n    \"research\": \"Read the engine handoff docs (API card, kits README, the stealth kit source) and the AI Hub session protocol README\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\n    \"usage\": \"unknown\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"Nine issues: five found by reading the code before the first run, four by the browser runs (a glyph that rendered as a question mark, a line that could not be flagged until its typing finished, the About screen opening over an open results panel, and the touch-only title still showing the keyboard hint). Also one harness failure that was not the game's: another agent's server held the port the harness used.\",\n    \"One critical issue, caught in review before any run: the Wire Room would have thrown on entry, because its helper was scoped inside the Corner. It was fixed; no player would have reached it.\",\n    \"The most helpful test was the AI Hub harness: it names every protocol check, and it passed for 2 players and for 4 players with latency, jitter and packet loss.\",\n    \"The least helpful test was the timed screenshot wait for attract mode: it took twelve seconds to show what the code already covered, and the Settings screenshot missed the Controller group until a DOM check.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"The stealth kit was the best part: guards with sight cones, takedowns and parries, and a HUD in one readable file. The Shell's results and settings screens, and the E.Dialog choices, worked without extra code.\",\n    \"I guessed on the stick inversion, since the settings screen has no axis option: I defined the toggles and wrapped the input in a proxy that inverts only pad sticks. I also guessed the weapon name for the guards and used 'sword', because the API card has no list of weapon names. E.Shell screens also stay open across scene changes, so the About screen opened over the results panel until I closed it by hand.\",\n    \"The most important things I built myself: the Wire Room minigame, the Animalese voices (a blip per letter, pitched per speaker), the scrolling About screen with the feedback text, the attract-mode route, and the AI Hub round glue.\",\n    \"The hardest part was the size: a 1.2 MB single file and a 66 KB API card. I read the stealth kit closely to learn the patterns, and a full clone of the repository filled the temporary disk, so I had to fetch only the handoff folder.\",\n    \"I would overhaul the Shell's screen lifetime: a screen should close when its scene changes, or the scene should be able to close it with one call.\",\n    \"I would add a built-in stick-inversion option to the Controller settings group, and a short list of the rig's weapon names in the API card.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The done criteria and the exit-interview shape were precise, and the links to the engine and hub README let me work from the real protocol rather than a guess.\",\n    \"The folder rules were unclear: the brief says to create the subfolder 'here' and to bundle the HTML 'in root', and I read 'root' as the work folder, where I put the HTML and the JSON. The brainstorm line says 50 titles and I produced 52. The Usage field asks for cost and token estimates that I could not measure.\",\n    \"The hardest thing was fitting the whole list into one pass: the controller-first UI with glyph swaps, attract mode, touch-only handling, the Wire Room, the multiplayer round, and the test tools. I did not test on a real gamepad or on real iOS Safari.\",\n    \"The test I would most like to run is a real DualSense in Chrome, to confirm that the two inversion toggles and the glyph swap feel right, followed by a four-player round across separate machines.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Wireframe Hour\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Stealth on the Corner. Listening in the Wire Room.\",\n    \"description\": \"A three-day Baltimore demo: steal a notebook off a stash-house roof past the corner boys, then flag the real lines in the wire room. Animalese voices, a choice that follows you, and a short timed round on the hub.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick or arrows move, Space jump, J strike, K block, C crouch, Shift sprint, L roll\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3d\", \"stealth\", \"retro\", \"exploration\", \"versus\", \"comedy\"]\n  }\n}\nEOF\nnode -e '\nconst j=JSON.parse(require(\"fs\").readFileSync(\"exit-interview.json\",\"utf8\"));\nconst empties=[]; const walk=(o,p)=>{ if(typeof o===\"string\"){ if(!o.trim() && p!==\"aiHub.series\") empties.push(p);} else if(Array.isArray(o)) o.forEach((x,i)=>walk(x,p+\"[\"+i+\"]\")); else if(o&&typeof o===\"object\") for(const k in o) walk(o[k],p?p+\".\"+k:k); };\nwalk(j,\"\"); console.log(\"parses; empty fields:\", empties.length?empties:\"none\"); console.log(\"tags:\", j.aiHub.tags.length, \"genre ok:\", [\"Stealth\"].includes(j.aiHub.genre));' && node build.mjs | tail -1","description":"Write final exit-interview JSON, validate it, and rebuild"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01N3pfzSUkJ7FTqcEZ5h889X":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium__20261102-190205__xac1bf5/work"}}}
