{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfC6GRMWi5b6h8L8mWV4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The demo is built and bundled as **Wireframe Hour**, a three-day Baltimore stealth and wiretap game. The file is `Wireframe-Hour-TheWire-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261102-190205.html` in the work folder root, with `exit-interview.json` beside it. Dev files are in the `Wireframe-Hour-TheWire-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261102-190205/` subfolder, and `build.mjs` there rebuilds the HTML.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Day 1, the Corner (stealth):** steal the notebook off a stash-house roof and leave by the east alley. Corner boys with sight cones, takedowns from behind or above, parry, roll, and crouching in grass. Built on the engine's stealth kit.\n- **Briefing:** Sgt. Rusk's dialogue ends in a choice (take the envelope or leave it). The choice changes one line in the Wire Room.\n- **Day 2, the Wire Room:** six intercepted calls. Move the cursor and flag the lines that matter (the corner, Thursday, the package, the re-up, and the envelope if you took it). Scoring counts real lines flagged, missed, and innocent lines flagged.\n- **Voices:** Animalese-style blips, pitched per speaker, with the words shown as subtitles. Music and sound effects are all code-written.\n- **Title and menus:** Play, Settings, Controls, About & Credits. Settings has invert-stick X and Y toggles and PlayStation or Xbox symbols.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 idle seconds the Corner plays itself, unhurt. Any input returns to the title.\n- **Touch-only devices:** the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" with Settings, and attract still runs.\n- **AI Hub:** parallel mode, 1–4 players. Each copy runs its own round, and the result is the time to the alley. A session skips the title and goes straight to the Corner.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- **Title:** I generated 52 candidates (the brief said 50) and picked \"Wireframe Hour\" with `crypto.randomInt`.\n- **Output location:** the brief says \"root\", and I read that as this work folder. The HTML and the JSON are there, not inside the subfolder.\n- **Engine:** the my-3D2dge handoff, inlined into the single HTML with no external dependencies.\n\n**Verification**\n- Headless Chromium: no page or console errors across the title, attract, briefing, Corner, Wire Room, results, About, game-over and Settings screens. A scored Wire Room run gave 4 real lines flagged, 3 missed, 0 innocent.\n- AI Hub harness: passes for 2 players and for 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss.\n- Viewport matrix in WebKit on the five phone profiles: clean.\n\n**Not verified**\n- Real gamepad: the invert toggles and the glyph switch are checked in code and through the settings API, not on a real DualSense.\n- Real iOS Safari, Firefox, Android and tablet/desktop sizes beyond the phone profiles.\n- The game-over and touch-only paths were checked only by rendering and code reading.\n- Shared-world mode is not implemented; the round is parallel only.\n- The Wire Room needs a fast mouse hover to be properly tested; keyboard works.\n- I added a read-only test hook (`window.__game`, `WIRE.state()`) for my tests. It's harmless but still in the file.\n- The subfolder also has copies of the hub's `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` for testing.\n\n---\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-02 19:02\n- **Source:** The Wire (2002-2008), created by David Simon and Ed Burns\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Stealth, with a listening minigame (adventure). Third-person 3/4 camera on the Corner (V cycles views); side view in the Wire Room\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1 engine (handoff package, single file, no dependencies); AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.3.0\n- **Art Direction:** engine-native pixel art on a PS1-style low-resolution buffer, brick rowhouses, dusk lighting\n- **Animation:** engine-native skeletal rigs (E.Humanoid), telegraphed attacks (E.Attack), takedown and parry actions\n- **Audio:** code-written chip-style songs in layers, synth sound effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices pitched per speaker\n- **Online:** AI Hub parallel mode (1–4 players). No other network use\n- **Testing:** headless Chromium (Playwright) for flow, scoring and screens; WebKit phone profiles via viewport-matrix; AI Hub harness for multiplayer\n- **Research:** engine handoff docs (API card, kits README, stealth kit source); AI Hub session protocol README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no estimates available)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** nine. Five came from reading the code before the first run, and four from the browser runs: a glyph rendering as \"?\", a line that couldn't be flagged until its typing finished, the About screen opening over an open results panel, and the touch-only title still showing the keyboard hint. A harness failure at one point was not the game's: another agent's server held the port.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** one, caught in review. The Wire Room would have thrown on entry because its helper was scoped inside the Corner. Fixed before any player could reach it.\n- **Most helpful test:** the AI Hub harness. It names every protocol check, and it passed for 2 and for 4 players under latency, jitter and loss.\n- **Least helpful test:** the timed screenshot wait for attract mode. It took 12 seconds to show what the code already covered. The Settings screenshot also missed the Controller group until a DOM check.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the stealth kit (guards with sight cones, takedowns, parries, a HUD) in one readable file. The Shell's results and settings screens and the E.Dialog choices worked without extra code.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** the settings screen has no axis-invert option, so I defined the toggles and wrapped the input in a proxy that inverts only pad sticks. I guessed the guards' weapon name and used `'sword'`, since the API card lists no weapon names. E.Shell screens also stay open across scene changes, so I had to close them by hand.\n- **Most important thing I built:** the Wire Room minigame, the Animalese voices, the scrolling About screen, the attract-mode route, and the AI Hub round glue.\n- **Hardest part:** the size. It's a 1.2 MB single file with a 66 KB API card. A full repository clone also filled the temp disk, so I fetched only the handoff folder.\n- **Overhaul:** the Shell's screen lifetime. A screen should close when its scene changes, or the scene should be able to close it with one call.\n- **One change:** a built-in stick-inversion option in the Controller settings group, and a short list of rig weapon names in the API card.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the done criteria and the exit-interview shape were precise, and the links to the engine and hub README let me work from the real protocol.\n- **Confusing:** \"in this folder's root\" versus the subfolder (I read \"root\" as the work folder). The brainstorm asked for 50 titles and I produced 52. The Usage field asks for cost and token estimates I couldn't measure.\n- **Hardest:** fitting the whole list into one pass: controller-first UI with glyph swaps, attract mode, touch-only handling, the Wire Room, the multiplayer round, and the test tools.\n- **Test I'd most like run:** a real DualSense in Chrome, to confirm the inversion toggles and the glyph swap feel right, then a four-player round across separate machines."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1095,"cache_read_input_tokens":222823,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1095,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41d7e7a4-7a0c-4c1d-8249-64635219af5f","uuid":"bc58c760-1fe9-46ea-86e4-a205eca29870","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:18:06.597Z","request_id":"req_011CgfC6G1JN5fecmWzW4Yp1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read TASK.md in the current folder and complete the task it describes. Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Most checkable parts are present: the bundled HTML exists in the work folder root, exit-interview.json parses and fills every field (4 testing, 6 engine and 4 prompt answers), and the final reply has all four sections. The feature requirement is not shown as met. TASK.md says the mouse should move focus on hover and click items wherever the genre uses it, but the Wire Room is keyboard-only and the reply says 'The Wire Room needs a fast mouse hover to be properly tested; keyboard works.' The invert-axis proxy was never exercised with a pad, and the reply lists the real-gamepad path as unverified. The transcript therefore does not show every TASK.md feature implemented and verified."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41d7e7a4-7a0c-4c1d-8249-64635219af5f","uuid":"220f421b-64b6-4382-9077-31ccf2b12471","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:18:22.311Z","isSynthetic":true}
